MCP Server Development
What it is
The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard for connecting AI agents to external tools, data sources, and services. An MCP server exposes tools (functions the agent can call), resources (data the agent can read), and prompts (reusable prompt templates) over a simple JSON-RPC transport. Building an MCP server lets you give any MCP-compatible AI client (Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, etc.) access to your own systems.
Why it matters
MCP solves the “N x M” integration problem: instead of building custom integrations between every AI client and every tool, you build one MCP server and it works with all MCP clients. This is the same pattern that LSP brought to editors. For personal infrastructure — like this Obsidian vault’s “brains” MCP server — it means your AI assistant can read, search, and write your notes through a well-defined interface.
Details
Architecture
MCP uses a client-server model over two transport types:

stdio: the client spawns the server as a subprocess and communicates over stdin/stdout. Best for local tools. This is what Claude Code and Claude Desktop use.
Streamable HTTP: the server runs as an HTTP endpoint using Server-Sent Events for server-to-client streaming. Best for remote/shared servers. Replaces the older SSE transport (deprecated in the 2025-03 spec revision).

The wire protocol is JSON-RPC 2.0. Messages are newline-delimited JSON over stdio, or HTTP POST/SSE over Streamable HTTP.
The protocol defines three capability types:

























CapabilityDirectionPurposeToolsClient calls serverFunctions the AI can invoke (search, write, compute)ResourcesClient reads serverData the AI can browse (files, DB records, API data)PromptsClient reads serverReusable prompt templates with parameters
TypeScript SDK
Install the MCP SDK:
npm install @modelcontextprotocol/sdk
Ensure your package.json has &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; — the SDK is ESM-only. See typescript-for-ai-tooling for project setup details.
Create a basic server:
import { McpServer } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js&quot;;
import { StdioServerTransport } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js&quot;;
import { z } from &quot;zod&quot;;

const server = new McpServer({
  name: &quot;my-server&quot;,
  version: &quot;1.0.0&quot;,
});

// Define a tool with Zod schema validation
server.tool(
  &quot;search_notes&quot;,
  &quot;Search knowledge base notes by query&quot;,
  {
    query: z.string().describe(&quot;Search query&quot;),
    limit: z.number().int().min(1).max(50).default(10).describe(&quot;Max results&quot;),
  },
  async ({ query, limit }) =&gt; {
    const results = await searchNotes(query, limit);
    return {
      content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: JSON.stringify(results, null, 2) }],
    };
  }
);

// Start the server
const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
The McpServer class is the high-level API. For more control, use the low-level Server class which gives direct access to request/notification handlers.
Defining tools
Each tool has:

name: unique identifier (snake_case by convention)
description: natural language description the AI reads to decide when to use the tool
inputSchema: JSON Schema or Zod schema defining the parameters
handler: async function that receives validated input and returns content blocks

Write descriptions as if explaining to a human colleague what the tool does and when to use it. The AI relies heavily on this description for tool selection.
server.tool(
  &quot;read_file&quot;,
  &quot;Read the contents of a file at the given path. Use this to examine note contents. &quot; +
  &quot;Returns the full file content as text. Fails if the file does not exist.&quot;,
  {
    path: z.string().describe(&quot;Relative path from vault root (e.g., &#039;knowledge/tech/mcp.md&#039;)&quot;),
  },
  async ({ path }) =&gt; {
    const safePath = resolveSafePath(path, vaultRoot);
    const content = await fs.readFile(safePath, &quot;utf-8&quot;);
    return { content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: content }] };
  }
);
Tool description best practices:

Explain when to use the tool, not just what it does
Mention what it returns and common failure modes
Keep it under ~200 words — the AI reads every tool description on every call
Use examples in the parameter descriptions (e.g., “e.g., ‘knowledge/tech/mcp.md’“)

Return types
Tools return content blocks. The most common types:
// Text (most common)
return { content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: &quot;result here&quot; }] };

// Multiple content blocks (e.g., text + image)
return {
  content: [
    { type: &quot;text&quot;, text: &quot;Here&#039;s the chart:&quot; },
    { type: &quot;image&quot;, data: base64String, mimeType: &quot;image/png&quot; },
  ],
};

// Error (set isError flag so the AI knows something went wrong)
return {
  content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: &quot;File not found: notes.md&quot; }],
  isError: true,
};
Always return errors as isError: true content blocks rather than throwing exceptions. This lets the AI see the error and recover, rather than crashing the tool call.
Defining resources
Resources represent data the AI can browse or read. They use URI templates:
server.resource(
  &quot;note&quot;,
  &quot;note://{path}&quot;,
  async (uri) =&gt; {
    const path = uri.pathname;
    const content = await fs.readFile(resolve(vaultRoot, path), &quot;utf-8&quot;);
    return {
      contents: [{ uri: uri.href, mimeType: &quot;text/markdown&quot;, text: content }],
    };
  }
);
Resources are useful for letting the AI discover and browse structured data (file trees, database records, API responses) without needing a tool call.
You can also define resource templates with list capabilities so the AI can discover available resources:
// List all notes as resources
server.resource(
  &quot;notes-list&quot;,
  &quot;note://list&quot;,
  async () =&gt; {
    const files = await glob(&quot;**/*.md&quot;, { cwd: vaultRoot });
    return {
      contents: [{
        uri: &quot;note://list&quot;,
        mimeType: &quot;application/json&quot;,
        text: JSON.stringify(files),
      }],
    };
  }
);
Defining prompts
Prompts are reusable templates that clients can present to users:
server.prompt(
  &quot;summarize_note&quot;,
  &quot;Summarize a knowledge base note&quot;,
  {
    path: z.string().describe(&quot;Path to the note to summarize&quot;),
  },
  async ({ path }) =&gt; {
    const content = await fs.readFile(resolve(vaultRoot, path), &quot;utf-8&quot;);
    return {
      messages: [
        {
          role: &quot;user&quot;,
          content: {
            type: &quot;text&quot;,
            text: `Please summarize this note concisely:\n\n${content}`,
          },
        },
      ],
    };
  }
);
Prompts are useful for standardizing common interactions — e.g., “summarize this note”, “review this code”, “generate a test plan”.
The filesystem MCP server pattern
A common pattern is wrapping a local directory (like an Obsidian vault) with an MCP server that provides:

read_file / write_file / edit_file — file CRUD
search_files — content search (grep-like)
list_directory / directory_tree — navigation
get_file_info — metadata (size, modified date)

This is exactly what the “brains” MCP server does for this vault. It constrains access to allowed directories and provides a safe interface for the AI to interact with the filesystem.
Anthropic provides @anthropic-ai/mcp-filesystem as a ready-made implementation of this pattern.
Security considerations
MCP servers run with the privileges of the user who starts them. Critical safeguards:

Path traversal prevention: resolve all paths against an allowed root and reject anything that escapes it. Use path.resolve() and verify the result starts with the allowed prefix. Watch out for symlinks — use fs.realpath() to resolve them before checking.

import { resolve, normalize } from &quot;node:path&quot;;
import { realpath } from &quot;node:fs/promises&quot;;

async function safePath(userPath: string, allowedRoot: string): Promise&lt;string&gt; {
  // Normalize to prevent ../ tricks
  const resolved = resolve(allowedRoot, normalize(userPath));

  // Check prefix before and after symlink resolution
  if (!resolved.startsWith(allowedRoot)) {
    throw new Error(&quot;Path traversal detected&quot;);
  }

  // Also check after resolving symlinks
  const real = await realpath(resolved);
  if (!real.startsWith(allowedRoot)) {
    throw new Error(&quot;Symlink escapes allowed directory&quot;);
  }

  return real;
}

Input validation: validate all tool inputs against their schemas before processing. Never trust that the AI sent valid data. Zod schemas in server.tool() handle this automatically.
Allowlisting: explicitly list which directories or operations are permitted. Deny by default.
Rate limiting: for remote servers, rate-limit tool calls to prevent abuse. Use middleware on the HTTP transport.
No secrets in tool outputs: be careful not to leak environment variables, credentials, or sensitive file contents through tool responses. Filter out .env files, *credentials*, etc.
Command injection: if your tool runs shell commands, never interpolate user input into command strings. Use execFile (not exec) and pass arguments as an array.

Error handling patterns
Robust error handling is critical — the AI will retry on errors, so make error messages informative:
server.tool(&quot;read_file&quot;, &quot;...&quot;, { path: z.string() }, async ({ path }) =&gt; {
  try {
    const safePath = await resolveSafePath(path, vaultRoot);
    const content = await fs.readFile(safePath, &quot;utf-8&quot;);
    return { content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: content }] };
  } catch (err) {
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === &quot;ENOENT&quot;) {
      return {
        content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: `File not found: ${path}. Use list_directory to see available files.` }],
        isError: true,
      };
    }
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === &quot;EACCES&quot;) {
      return {
        content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: `Permission denied: ${path}` }],
        isError: true,
      };
    }
    // Re-throw unexpected errors
    throw err;
  }
});
Tips:

Suggest recovery actions in error messages (“Use list_directory to see available files”)
Distinguish between user errors (return isError) and server bugs (throw)
Log errors server-side for debugging but don’t expose stack traces to the client

Testing MCP servers
MCP Inspector: an official tool for interactively testing MCP servers. Run it against your server to list tools, call them, and inspect responses:
npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector node ./dist/index.js
This opens a web UI where you can browse tools, fill in parameters, and see raw JSON-RPC messages. Invaluable during development.
Manual testing via stdio: pipe JSON-RPC messages directly:
# Initialize the connection (required first step)
echo &#039;{&quot;jsonrpc&quot;:&quot;2.0&quot;,&quot;id&quot;:1,&quot;method&quot;:&quot;initialize&quot;,&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;protocolVersion&quot;:&quot;2024-11-05&quot;,&quot;capabilities&quot;:{},&quot;clientInfo&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;test&quot;,&quot;version&quot;:&quot;1.0.0&quot;}}}&#039; | node ./dist/index.js

# List tools
echo &#039;{&quot;jsonrpc&quot;:&quot;2.0&quot;,&quot;id&quot;:1,&quot;method&quot;:&quot;tools/list&quot;}&#039; | node ./dist/index.js
Note: you must send an initialize request before any other method. The server will reject requests until initialization is complete.
Automated tests: import your tool handlers directly and test them as regular async functions, bypassing the transport layer:
import { describe, it, expect } from &quot;vitest&quot;;
import { searchNotes } from &quot;./tools/search.js&quot;;

describe(&quot;search_notes&quot;, () =&gt; {
  it(&quot;returns matching notes&quot;, async () =&gt; {
    const results = await searchNotes(&quot;typescript&quot;, 10);
    expect(results).toHaveLength(2);
    expect(results[0].path).toContain(&quot;typescript&quot;);
  });
});
End-to-end tests: spin up the server in-process and connect a client to it:
import { McpServer } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js&quot;;
import { Client } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js&quot;;
import { InMemoryTransport } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/inMemory.js&quot;;

const [clientTransport, serverTransport] = InMemoryTransport.createLinkedPair();
const server = createMyServer();
const client = new Client({ name: &quot;test&quot;, version: &quot;1.0.0&quot; }, {});

await server.connect(serverTransport);
await client.connect(clientTransport);

const result = await client.callTool({ name: &quot;search_notes&quot;, arguments: { query: &quot;test&quot; } });
expect(result.content[0].text).toContain(&quot;test&quot;);
Deployment

Local (stdio): configure in your MCP client’s settings. For Claude Code, add to .mcp.json at the project root:

{
  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {
    &quot;brains&quot;: {
      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,
      &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;-y&quot;, &quot;@anthropic-ai/mcp-filesystem&quot;],
      &quot;env&quot;: {}
    }
  }
}
For Claude Desktop, add to ~/.config/claude/claude_desktop_config.json (Linux) or ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (macOS):
{
  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {
    &quot;brains&quot;: {
      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;node&quot;,
      &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;/absolute/path/to/dist/index.js&quot;],
      &quot;env&quot;: {
        &quot;VAULT_PATH&quot;: &quot;/var/home/oxzo/coding/brains&quot;
      }
    }
  }
}

Remote (Streamable HTTP): deploy as a standard HTTP server (e.g., on a VPS, behind a reverse proxy). Add authentication middleware:

import { StreamableHTTPServerTransport } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js&quot;;
import express from &quot;express&quot;;

const app = express();

// Authentication middleware
app.use(&quot;/mcp&quot;, (req, res, next) =&gt; {
  const token = req.headers.authorization(&quot;Bearer &quot;, &quot;&quot;);
  if (token !== process.env.MCP_AUTH_TOKEN) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: &quot;Unauthorized&quot; });
  }
  next();
});

const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });
app.post(&quot;/mcp&quot;, transport.handleRequest.bind(transport));
await server.connect(transport);

app.listen(3000);
Logging and debugging
MCP servers communicate over stdio, which means you cannot use console.log for debugging — it would corrupt the JSON-RPC stream. Options:
// Write to stderr (doesn&#039;t interfere with stdio transport)
console.error(&quot;Debug: processing request&quot;, toolName);

// Use the MCP SDK&#039;s built-in logging
server.server.sendLoggingMessage({
  level: &quot;info&quot;,
  data: &quot;Processing search request&quot;,
});

// Write to a file
import { appendFileSync } from &quot;node:fs&quot;;
function debug(msg: string) {
  appendFileSync(&quot;/tmp/mcp-debug.log&quot;, `${new Date().toISOString()} ${msg}\n`);
}
Real-world example: the “brains” MCP server
This Obsidian vault is served to Claude via an MCP filesystem server. It provides tools like read_file, write_file, search_files, list_directory, and edit_file — all scoped to the vault directory. This is why Claude can read and write notes in this vault directly, creating a persistent shared memory between sessions.
The configuration lives in the project’s .mcp.json and connects via stdio transport.
Common pitfalls

Forgetting ESM setup: the MCP SDK is ESM-only. Ensure &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; in package.json and &quot;module&quot;: &quot;Node16&quot; in tsconfig.json. See typescript-for-ai-tooling for details.
Writing to stdout: any console.log in an stdio server corrupts the transport. Use console.error or file logging instead.
Tool descriptions too vague: “Searches files” is not enough. The AI needs to know what kinds of files, what the query format is, and what the output looks like.
Not handling large outputs: if a tool can return megabytes of data (e.g., reading a huge file), truncate or paginate. Large responses waste the AI’s context window.
Missing error context: &quot;Error occurred&quot; is useless. Return &quot;File not found: path/to/file. Available files: [list]. Use list_directory to browse.&quot; — give the AI enough context to self-correct.
Synchronous operations blocking the event loop: all tool handlers should be async. Use fs/promises (not fs), and avoid CPU-heavy synchronous work.

Related

../ai/model-context-protocol
typescript-for-ai-tooling
../ai/agentic-ai-architecture

Sources

MCP specification: spec.modelcontextprotocol.io
MCP TypeScript SDK: github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk
MCP Inspector: github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector
Anthropic MCP documentation: docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/mcp
MCP filesystem server: github.com/anthropics/anthropic-quickstarts/tree/main/mcp-filesystem

tech/typescript-for-ai-tooling.content:
TypeScript for AI Tooling
What it is
Patterns and practices for building AI-powered tools, agents, and integrations in TypeScript. TypeScript has become the de facto language for AI application development — the Anthropic SDK, OpenAI SDK, Vercel AI SDK, and MCP SDK are all TypeScript-first, and the type system maps naturally onto tool schemas and structured outputs.
Why it matters
Building AI tools requires handling complex, semi-structured data flows: tool schemas, streaming responses, multi-turn conversations, and error recovery. TypeScript’s type system catches schema mismatches at compile time, and its async/streaming primitives fit the event-driven nature of LLM APIs. If you’re building agents, MCP servers, or AI-enhanced CLIs, TypeScript is the most productive choice.
Details
Project setup
Recommended tsconfig.json for AI tooling projects:
{
  &quot;compilerOptions&quot;: {
    &quot;target&quot;: &quot;ES2022&quot;,
    &quot;module&quot;: &quot;Node16&quot;,
    &quot;moduleResolution&quot;: &quot;Node16&quot;,
    &quot;outDir&quot;: &quot;./dist&quot;,
    &quot;rootDir&quot;: &quot;./src&quot;,
    &quot;strict&quot;: true,
    &quot;esModuleInterop&quot;: true,
    &quot;skipLibCheck&quot;: true,
    &quot;declaration&quot;: true,
    &quot;sourceMap&quot;: true
  },
  &quot;include&quot;: [&quot;src/**/*&quot;]
}
Key choices: &quot;module&quot;: &quot;Node16&quot; for proper ESM support (important — the MCP SDK and Anthropic SDK both ship as ESM). Set &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; in package.json to match.
For rapid iteration during development, use tsx instead of tsc + node:
npm install -D tsx
npx tsx src/index.ts          # run directly, no build step
npx tsx watch src/index.ts    # auto-restart on changes
SDK setup
Install the Anthropic SDK:
npm install @anthropic-ai/sdk
Initialize the client:
import Anthropic from &quot;@anthropic-ai/sdk&quot;;

const client = new Anthropic({
  apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, // or set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY env var
});
The SDK auto-reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment if no key is passed.
The SDK includes built-in retry logic with exponential backoff for transient errors (429 rate limits, 500 server errors). You can configure this:
const client = new Anthropic({
  maxRetries: 3,       // default is 2
  timeout: 600_000,    // ms, default is 10 minutes
});
Tool / function calling
Define tools using JSON Schema (the API’s native format) or generate schemas from Zod:
import { z } from &quot;zod&quot;;
import { zodToJsonSchema } from &quot;zod-to-json-schema&quot;;

const SearchParamsSchema = z.object({
  query: z.string().describe(&quot;Search query&quot;),
  limit: z.number().int().min(1).max(50).default(10),
});

const tools: Anthropic.Tool[] = [
  {
    name: &quot;search_notes&quot;,
    description: &quot;Search the knowledge base for notes matching a query&quot;,
    input_schema: zodToJsonSchema(SearchParamsSchema) as Anthropic.Tool.InputSchema,
  },
];
Handle tool_use responses by dispatching to handler functions:
type ToolHandler = (input: unknown) =&gt; Promise&lt;string&gt;;

const handlers: Record&lt;string, ToolHandler&gt; = {
  search_notes: async (input) =&gt; {
    const params = SearchParamsSchema.parse(input);
    const results = await searchNotes(params.query, params.limit);
    return JSON.stringify(results);
  },
};
Pitfall: zodToJsonSchema wraps the output in a $schema property and definitions key by default. Pass { $refStrategy: &quot;none&quot; } to flatten it if the API rejects the schema:
zodToJsonSchema(SearchParamsSchema, { $refStrategy: &quot;none&quot; })
Streaming patterns
Use the streaming API for real-time UI updates:
const stream = client.messages.stream({
  model: &quot;claude-sonnet-4-20250514&quot;,
  max_tokens: 4096,
  messages,
  tools,
});

for await (const event of stream) {
  if (event.type === &quot;content_block_delta&quot;) {
    if (event.delta.type === &quot;text_delta&quot;) {
      process.stdout.write(event.delta.text);
    }
  }
}

const finalMessage = await stream.finalMessage();
For tool use with streaming, you also need to handle input_json_delta events — the tool input arrives as incremental JSON chunks:
for await (const event of stream) {
  if (event.type === &quot;content_block_delta&quot;) {
    if (event.delta.type === &quot;input_json_delta&quot;) {
      // Partial JSON string — accumulate until content_block_stop
    }
  }
}
// Or just use stream.finalMessage() which assembles everything
For server-sent events (SSE) in web apps, pipe the stream through a ReadableStream to the client. Libraries like Vercel AI SDK abstract this further with streamText() and framework-specific helpers.
Agentic loops
The core pattern for an agent: send messages, check for tool use, execute tools, feed results back:
async function agentLoop(
  client: Anthropic,
  messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[],
  tools: Anthropic.Tool[],
): Promise&lt;string&gt; {
  const MAX_ITERATIONS = 20;

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; MAX_ITERATIONS; i++) {
    const response = await client.messages.create({
      model: &quot;claude-sonnet-4-20250514&quot;,
      max_tokens: 4096,
      messages,
      tools,
    });

    // Collect the assistant&#039;s response
    messages.push({ role: &quot;assistant&quot;, content: response.content });

    // If no tool use, we&#039;re done
    if (response.stop_reason !== &quot;tool_use&quot;) {
      const textBlock = response.content.find((b) =&gt; b.type === &quot;text&quot;);
      return textBlock?.text ?? &quot;&quot;;
    }

    // Execute each tool call
    const toolResults: Anthropic.ToolResultBlockParam[] = [];
    for (const block of response.content) {
      if (block.type === &quot;tool_use&quot;) {
        try {
          const result = await handlers[block.name](block.input);
          toolResults.push({
            type: &quot;tool_result&quot;,
            tool_use_id: block.id,
            content: result,
          });
        } catch (err) {
          toolResults.push({
            type: &quot;tool_result&quot;,
            tool_use_id: block.id,
            content: `Error: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`,
            is_error: true,
          });
        }
      }
    }

    messages.push({ role: &quot;user&quot;, content: toolResults });
  }

  throw new Error(&quot;Agent exceeded maximum iterations&quot;);
}
Key design decisions:

Max iterations: always cap the loop to prevent runaway costs. 20 is a reasonable default; adjust based on task complexity.
Error as tool result: return errors back to the model as is_error: true tool results so it can self-correct. Never throw from a tool handler — the agent loop should keep running.
Retry logic: the SDK handles 429 retries automatically. For your own transient failures (network, database), add retry logic in the tool handler itself.
Parallel tool calls: the model can return multiple tool_use blocks in one response. The loop above processes them sequentially, but you can use Promise.all for independent tools:

const toolResults = await Promise.all(
  response.content
    .filter((b): b is Anthropic.ToolUseBlock =&gt; b.type === &quot;tool_use&quot;)
    .map(async (block) =&gt; {
      try {
        const result = await handlers[block.name](block.input);
        return { type: &quot;tool_result&quot; as const, tool_use_id: block.id, content: result };
      } catch (err) {
        return {
          type: &quot;tool_result&quot; as const,
          tool_use_id: block.id,
          content: `Error: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`,
          is_error: true,
        };
      }
    })
);
System prompt patterns
Good system prompts for agents are structured and explicit. Common patterns:
const systemPrompt = `You are a knowledge base assistant. You have access to tools for searching and reading notes.

## Rules
- Always search before answering factual questions
- Cite the note path when referencing specific content
- If a search returns no results, say so — don&#039;t hallucinate

## Available context
- Vault root: /knowledge
- Note format: Markdown with YAML frontmatter
`;
For multi-tool agents, describe the tool selection strategy in the system prompt rather than relying on tool descriptions alone.
Structured output
Force JSON output by using a tool with a strict schema, then extracting the result:
const response = await client.messages.create({
  model: &quot;claude-sonnet-4-20250514&quot;,
  max_tokens: 1024,
  messages: [{ role: &quot;user&quot;, content: &quot;Classify this text...&quot; }],
  tools: [{
    name: &quot;classify&quot;,
    description: &quot;Return the classification result&quot;,
    input_schema: zodToJsonSchema(ClassificationSchema) as Anthropic.Tool.InputSchema,
  }],
  tool_choice: { type: &quot;tool&quot;, name: &quot;classify&quot; },
});

const toolBlock = response.content.find((b) =&gt; b.type === &quot;tool_use&quot;);
const result = ClassificationSchema.parse(toolBlock?.input);
Using tool_choice with a specific tool name forces the model to call that tool, effectively giving you structured output. This is more reliable than asking for JSON in the prompt.
For complex schemas, prefer flat structures over deeply nested ones — the model produces more accurate output. If you need nested data, consider splitting into multiple tool calls.
MCP client integration
Connect to MCP servers from TypeScript using the MCP client SDK. See mcp-server-development for the server side.
import { Client } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/index.js&quot;;
import { StdioClientTransport } from &quot;@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/stdio.js&quot;;

const transport = new StdioClientTransport({
  command: &quot;node&quot;,
  args: [&quot;./my-mcp-server/dist/index.js&quot;],
});

const client = new Client({ name: &quot;my-app&quot;, version: &quot;1.0.0&quot; }, {});
await client.connect(transport);

const { tools } = await client.listTools();
const result = await client.callTool({
  name: &quot;search_notes&quot;,
  arguments: { query: &quot;typescript&quot; },
});
To bridge MCP tools into an Anthropic API call, convert the MCP tool list to the Anthropic tool format:
const anthropicTools: Anthropic.Tool[] = tools.map((t) =&gt; ({
  name: t.name,
  description: t.description ?? &quot;&quot;,
  input_schema: t.inputSchema as Anthropic.Tool.InputSchema,
}));
Token management and context windows
Large agent conversations can exceed context limits. Strategies:

Summarize older messages: periodically ask the model to summarize the conversation so far, replace old messages with the summary.
Truncate tool results: large tool outputs (file contents, search results) can dominate the context. Truncate or summarize before pushing into messages.
Track token counts: use the usage field from each response to track cumulative context size.

function estimateTokens(text: string): number {
  // Rough estimate: 1 token ≈ 4 characters for English text
  return Math.ceil(text.length / 4);
}
For precise counts, use the Anthropic token counting endpoint or the @anthropic-ai/tokenizer package.
Testing AI code


Mock API responses: create fixtures of Anthropic.Message objects and stub the client. The SDK types make this straightforward:
const mockResponse: Anthropic.Message = {
  id: &quot;msg_test&quot;,
  type: &quot;message&quot;,
  role: &quot;assistant&quot;,
  model: &quot;claude-sonnet-4-20250514&quot;,
  content: [{ type: &quot;text&quot;, text: &quot;Hello&quot; }],
  stop_reason: &quot;end_turn&quot;,
  stop_sequence: null,
  usage: { input_tokens: 10, output_tokens: 5 },
};


Snapshot testing: snapshot tool schemas to catch accidental changes. Zod schemas make this easy since they serialize to JSON Schema deterministically.


Integration tests: use a cheap model (e.g., Haiku) with real API calls for end-to-end agent loop tests. Set low max_tokens to keep costs down.


Deterministic seeds: not yet available in the Anthropic API, so integration tests will have some variance. Assert on structure, not exact text.


Vitest/Jest setup: for projects using tsx, configure Vitest with globals: true and use vi.mock() to stub the Anthropic client.


Cost tracking
Track token usage from every API response:
const response = await client.messages.create({ ... });
const { input_tokens, output_tokens } = response.usage;

// Approximate cost (check current pricing — as of 2026-06)
// Sonnet 4.6: $3/MTok input, $15/MTok output
// Haiku 4.5:  $1/MTok input, $5/MTok output
// Opus 4.8:   $5/MTok input, $25/MTok output
const cost = (input_tokens / 1_000_000) * inputPricePerMillion
           + (output_tokens / 1_000_000) * outputPricePerMillion;
Log cumulative tokens per conversation to catch runaway agents early. Set cost budgets in your agent loop:
let totalCost = 0;
const BUDGET = 1.0; // $1 max per conversation

for (let i = 0; i &lt; MAX_ITERATIONS; i++) {
  const response = await client.messages.create({ ... });
  const iterCost = computeCost(response.usage);
  totalCost += iterCost;

  if (totalCost &gt; BUDGET) {
    throw new Error(`Agent exceeded budget: $${totalCost.toFixed(4)}`);
  }
  // ... rest of loop
}
Common pitfalls

Forgetting &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; in package.json: the MCP and Anthropic SDKs use ESM. Without this, you get ERR_REQUIRE_ESM errors.
Not handling stop_reason: &quot;max_tokens&quot;: if the model hits the token limit mid-response, it stops abruptly. Check for this and either increase max_tokens or prompt the model to continue.
Mutating the messages array outside the loop: the agent loop owns the messages array. If you modify it from another part of your code, you can corrupt the conversation state.
Huge tool results: returning a 100KB file as a tool result wastes context window. Truncate, summarize, or paginate large outputs.
Not validating tool input: always parse tool inputs through Zod (or equivalent) before using them. The model can send malformed data, especially with complex schemas.

